# cppcheck suppressions for the `cppcheck` CI job (#95).

# Format: one `id:file` per line (cppcheck --suppressions-list). Every
# entry carries the reason it is not a defect. An entry is scoped to one
# check in one file, never a check across the tree: a new finding of the
# same kind in a different file still fails the job. A new finding of the
# same kind in an already-listed file is hidden by its entry; review
# changes to a listed file with that in mind.

# The job runs with --enable=information, so an entry that no longer
# matches anything in a file that is still analysed is itself reported
# (unmatchedSuppression) and fails the job. An entry naming a file that
# was renamed or deleted is not reported; drop it in the same change.

# Written against cppcheck 2.13.0, the ubuntu-24.04 apt package the job
# pins. Other versions find a different set; regenerate against 2.13.0
# (see the job in .github/workflows/ci.yml for the exact command).

# Real defects are fixed, not listed here. The first pass (#95) found
# three: the alert detail panel printing last_seen as "First seen"
# (two localtime() results aliasing one static buffer), %u used for an
# int protocol number in capture.c, and a NULL check in
# http_log_record() that the next statement contradicted.

# ── Tool configuration, not findings ────────────────────────────────────
# System headers are deliberately not on the include path; cppcheck's
# std/posix library configs model libc instead, which is its documented
# recommended setup. Emitted once per #include <...>.
missingIncludeSystem
# beacon_parse_ies() carried a checkLevelNormal entry here until #127:
# moving its SSID, PHY-tier and vendor-OUI logic into src/dot11_ie.c
# brought it back under the normal-level ValueFlow budget, and the
# unmatched suppression then failed the job, so the entry was retired.
# main() went over the normal-level budget on 2026-10-05, when #82's --tune
# parsing landed on top of #82's three --wps-* flags. Both are ordinary
# argv branches; the function is simply long (arg parsing is nearly all
# of it), and cppcheck's normal-level ValueFlow gives up rather than
# finding anything.
# Honest limit on this entry, stated rather than implied: unlike the
# retired beacon_snoop.c entry above, it is NOT backed by a one-off
# --check-level=exhaustive run confirming the normal level missed
# nothing. cppcheck is not installed on the dev host and the job pins
# 2.13.0, so that cross-check could not be run here. Worth doing, with
# splitting argv handling out of main() as the real fix — that would
# retire this entry instead of carrying it.
checkLevelNormal:src/main.c

# ── readdir() ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# cppcheck recommends readdir_r(), which glibc deprecated in 2.24 and
# POSIX.1-2024 marks obsolescent. readdir() is thread-safe when each
# thread walks its own DIR*, which every caller here does (a DIR* from a
# local opendir()/fdopendir(), closed in the same function).
readdirCalled:src/capture/probe.c
readdirCalled:src/platform/linux_pid.c
readdirCalled:src/platform/linux_dhcp.c
# The #87 retention sweep: sweep_locked() fdopendir()s a dup of the pinned
# export directory descriptor into a local DIR* and closedir()s it before
# returning. Reached only from the main thread (startup, then the poll
# loop via eapol_sweep_due()) and only under the module mutex.
readdirCalled:src/eapol_log.c
# #121's fd count: count_fds() opendir()s /proc/self/fd into a local DIR*
# and closedir()s it before returning; nothing else ever sees that stream.
readdirCalled:src/sensor_health.c
readdirCalled:research/ingest/main.c

# ── False positives, checked by hand ────────────────────────────────────
# Bucket arrays filled field-by-field when a slot is claimed; the flagged
# reads are sizeof() operands and snprintf() destinations of that claim.
legacyUninitvar:src/alerts/rules_wired.c
# rows_data[rc].val is the snprintf() destination, written before read.
legacyUninitvar:src/views/dashboard_grid.c
# order[0] is read only when top > 0, which implies g_info_n > 0 and the
# preceding loop initialised it.
uninitvar:src/views/dashboard_bands.c
# tmp[] is filled by dfs_append(); cppcheck does not follow the out-param.
uninitvar:src/views/procs.c
# qsort() of sorted_exts with count sec_c_next, all of which were written.
uninitvar:src/tls_log.c
# fdopen() failure leaves fd open (POSIX), so the close(fd) on that path
# is the only close, not a second one.
doubleFree:src/secure_file.c
# Band heights / panel widths start equal on purpose; each is a separate
# layout knob so one can be tuned without touching the others.
duplicateAssignExpression:src/views/dashboard.c
